<description>
Complete partial paths inside a framed rectangle by projecting external color traces inward.

Input structure:
- bg, a 1px rectangular frame (border color) with uniform interior fill.
- Colored components outside the frame touching its edges.

Rule:
- Output = the frame's interior, filled with the fill color.
- Map each outside component's frame-touch points into interior coordinates.
- Monochrome components become solid Manhattan stripes between mapped endpoints.
- Two-color components become alternating-color Manhattan paths.

Key insight: Endpoint colors of two-color traces anchor the alternating phase deterministically.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "touch point s on closed rectangular frame where an open rectangular frame touches it",
  "dst": "connect to another touch point d on closed rectangular frame where an open rectangular frame touches it",
  "path": "path to complete the open rectangular frame",
  "trajectory": "same color and pattern as the open rectangular frame",
  "grid_shape": "crop to only retain inner area of the closed rectangular frame"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 20-30 rows x 20-30 cols, uniform bg color
  closed_frame:
    count: 1 (largest by area)
    border: 1px complete rectangle, color bc != bg; bc=3 [observed]
    fill: uniform interior, color != bc; may equal bg
    interior: 4-13 rows x 7-14 cols
    margin: >=4 cells from each grid edge [observed]
  open_rects:
    count: 2-3 4-connected components touching the closed frame's ring (cells adjacent to border)
    shape: open rectangular frame with 2 full sides meeting at a corner + partial stubs of the other 2 sides ending at the ring
    type_L (2 full sides): free ends touch 2 different frame sides; produces L-shaped path inside interior
    type_U (3 full sides, observed in 1/4 examples): free ends touch same frame side; paired with matching U on opposite side -> full-span stripes
    color: 1-color (monochrome) or 2-color (alternating along perimeter); colors != bg, bc, fill
    interior: bg-filled
    no overlap: open rects do not overlap each other or the closed frame
  distractors: 0-2 small blobs (1-4 cells) not touching ring [observed in 1/4 examples]
  output:
    shape: closed frame interior dimensions
    base: fill color
    paths: Manhattan L-path (or straight) connecting projected free-end positions
    mono: solid color along path
    alternating: 2-color alternation, phase anchored by free-end colors from ring cells
    elbow: at position corresponding to the missing corner; chosen so corner is strictly inside interior (not on edge)

generation_strategy: |
  Path-first: build output interior with paths, then construct open rects outside.
  1. Pick grid size [20..30]^2, bg, bc (!=bg), fill (!=bc, may==bg). Pick interior H in [4..13], W in [7..14]. Place closed frame with >=4 margin to grid edges.
  2. For each of K=2-3 open rects:
     a. Pick 1 or 2 colors from palette \ {bg,bc,fill}. Pick 2 frame sides (adjacent for L-shape).
     b. Pick free-end positions: one on each chosen side of the interior boundary.
     c. Draw Manhattan L-path inside interior connecting endpoints; elbow at the corner that is strictly inside. For 2-color: alternate, anchor phase to endpoint colors.
     d. Project endpoints outward through the frame border to get ring touch points.
     e. Build external open rect: choose a corner position outside the frame, draw 2 full sides + 2 partial stubs ending at the touch points. Color mono or alternating matching the path.
     f. Verify no overlap with other components or grid edges.
  3. Optionally add 0-2 small distractor blobs >=2 cells from frame border.
  4. Fill remaining cells with bg. Validate solver(input) == output.
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

